player_character: The fog wasn't supposed to be here. Not this thick, not this fast.
player_character: One second I'm following the ribbon my Kuya tied on the branches — our trail markers, leading back down to camp — and the next, the whole mountain just swallows itself in white.
player_character: And it got cold. Not mountain-cold. Not the kind you get up in Sagada in December. Cold like something was breathing right behind my neck, waiting to see if I'd notice.
player_character: 	I shouted for my Tito Rey. My own voice came back thin, like the fog chewed it up before it even left my mouth.
player_character: That's when I saw the trees weren't right anymore. Still shaped like the same acacias, the same narra, but the bark looked wet. Grey.
player_character:  And I swear — I swear — there were shapes moving inside the trunks. Not carvings. Just there for a second, gone the moment I looked straight at them.
player_character: My Lola used to tell us this. Every camping trip, every barrio procession, every time we passed a big old balete tree on the road, she'd say —  "huwag kang dadaan diyan nang hindi nagpapaalam".  Always say "tabi-tabi po" so you don't step on something you can't see.
player_character: I always figured that was just something you tell kids to keep them behaving in the dark.
player_character: I don't think it was just that anymore.
player_character: My phone won't even turn on. Not low battery — just nothing, like it forgot it was ever a phone.
player_character: And the little compass Papa gave me before this trip? Needle's just spinning, slow, like it can't decide which way is anywhere.
player_character: This isn't an ordinary kind of lost. This is something older than the trail, older than the mountain having a name at all. Like I walked into a place people aren't supposed to enter.
player_character: Okay. Okay, just breathe. Panicking won't get me home.
player_character: Papa always said — when you're lost, stop first, don't just start running, think before you move.
player_character: I don't think that rule was made for a forest that moves on its own.
player_character: But it's all I've got. So — one step, then another. Find the way back. Whatever's wearing the shape of these trees to keep me here, whatever's watching me from between the fog — I'm not staying lost here.
player_character: I just need to figure out what "home" still means, from inside this.